{"task_id": "Mbpp/57", "solution": "def find_Max_Num(digits: list[int]) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Forms the largest possible number from a given list of digits.\n    Converts digits to strings, sorts them in descending order, joins them, and converts the result to an integer.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not digits:\n        return 0\n\n    # Convert digits to strings to enable string-based sorting\n    str_digits = [str(d) for d in digits]\n\n    # Sort the string digits in descending order\n    # For example, [1, 2, 3] becomes [\"3\", \"2\", \"1\"]\n    # For cases like [3, 30], \"30\" < \"3\" in lexicographical sort, so we need a custom comparator for complex cases,\n    # but the problem statement `assert find_Max_Num([1,2,3]) == 321` implies simple descending sort for single digits.\n    # The current design context (Sequence Diagram step 4) confirms \"Sort string digits in descending order\".\n    # This implies a simple string sort for forming the largest number from given digits, not handling cases like [3, 30].\n    # Based on the assert, the input digits are intended to be single digits (0-9).\n    str_digits.sort(reverse=True)\n\n    # Join the sorted string digits to form the largest number string\n    largest_num_str = \"\".join(str_digits)\n\n    # Convert the resulting string back to an integer\n    return int(largest_num_str)"}
