{"task_id": "Mbpp/111", "solution": "def common_in_nested_lists(nested_lists: list[list]) -> set:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the common elements in a given collection of nested lists.\n    An element is considered common if it exists in every list within the nested lists.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not nested_lists:\n        return set()\n\n    common_set = set(nested_lists[0])\n\n    for sublist in nested_lists[1:]:\n        common_set.intersection_update(sublist)\n        # Optimization: if common_set becomes empty, no more common elements can be found\n        if not common_set:\n            break\n\n    return common_set"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/119", "solution": "def search(arr: list[int]) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the single element that appears only once in a sorted array\n    where all other elements appear an even number of times.\n    \"\"\"\n    left, right = 0, len(arr) - 1\n\n    while left <= right:\n        mid = (left + right) // 2\n\n        # If mid is the unique element\n        # It's unique if it's not equal to its neighbors,\n        # or if it's at an edge and not equal to its only neighbor.\n        is_unique = True\n        if mid > 0 and arr[mid] == arr[mid - 1]:\n            is_unique = False\n        if mid < len(arr) - 1 and arr[mid] == arr[mid + 1]:\n            is_unique = False\n\n        if is_unique:\n            return arr[mid]\n\n        # Determine which half to search\n        # If mid is part of a pair (arr[mid] == arr[mid+1])\n        if mid < len(arr) - 1 and arr[mid] == arr[mid + 1]:\n            # If the number of elements before this pair (including this pair) is even,\n            # the unique element is in the right half.\n            # Number of elements is (mid - 0 + 1) -> (mid + 1)\n            # If (mid + 1) is even, it means arr[0]...arr[mid] contains an even number of elements.\n            # So (mid + 1) is the count for the first element of the pair.\n            # The pair spans arr[mid] and arr[mid+1].\n            # The count of elements from index 0 to mid+1 is (mid+2).\n            # If (mid+2) is even, unique element is to the right.\n            # E.g., [1,1,2,2,3,4,4], mid=0 (arr[0]=1, arr[1]=1), count = 2 (even), search right.\n            # E.g., [1,1,2,3,3], mid=0 (arr[0]=1, arr[1]=1), count = 2 (even), search right.\n            if (mid % 2) == 0:  # If mid is even, then arr[mid] is the first element of a pair\n                left = mid + 2 # Skip the pair arr[mid], arr[mid+1]\n            else: # If mid is odd, then arr[mid] is the second element of a pair\n                right = mid - 1 # The unique element must be before this pair\n        # If mid is part of a pair (arr[mid] == arr[mid-1])\n        elif mid > 0 and arr[mid] == arr[mid - 1]:\n            # If the number of elements before this element (including this element) is odd,\n            # the unique element is in the right half.\n            # The pair spans arr[mid-1] and arr[mid].\n            # The count of elements from index 0 to mid is (mid+1).\n            # If (mid+1) is odd, unique element is to the right.\n            # E.g., [1,2,2,3,3], mid=2 (arr[2]=2, arr[1]=2), count = 3 (odd), search right.\n            if (mid % 2) == 0: # If mid is even, then arr[mid] is the second element of a pair\n                right = mid - 2 # Skip the pair arr[mid-1], arr[mid]\n            else: # If mid is odd, then arr[mid] is the first element of a pair\n                left = mid + 1 # The unique element must be after this pair\n\n    return -1 # Should not reach here if an unique element is guaranteed to exist."}
